A 61-year-old male with long-standing type 2 diabetes mellitus, hypertension, and stage 4 chronic kidney disease (baseline serum creatinine 2.5 mg/dL, eGFR 22 mL/min/1.73 m2, serum urate 9.6 mg/dL) presents to the nephrology clinic. Over the past 12 months, he has suffered three debilitating gout flares involving his left first metatarsophalangeal joint and right knee; physical examination reveals a firm, chalky tophus on his left olecranon process. Four days ago, at the onset of his third flare, the patient self-treated by consuming over-the-counter naproxen (500 mg twice daily). On evaluation today, his left knee remains intensely red, hot, and swollen, and laboratory testing reveals an acute creatinine rise to 3.4 mg/dL (AKI superimposed on CKD). The clinical team must formulate an evidence-based, comprehensive inpatient and outpatient management plan that resolves the acute flare, reverses the hemodynamic AKI, safely lowers serum urate below 6.0 mg/dL, and prevents recurrent destabilization attacks.

Trace the complete 5-step clinical, pharmacological, and physiological decision-making sequence required to successfully manage this patient across the entire disease course:
1. The immediate diagnosis and intrarenal mechanism of his acute creatinine spike (NSAID-induced hemodynamic AKI) and its mandatory initial management;
2. The selection and rationale of the optimal acute anti-inflammatory flare therapy appropriate for an active monoarthritis in stage 4 CKD with superimposed AKI;
3. The evaluation of criteria for initiating long-term urate-lowering therapy (ULT) in this patient and the precise target serum urate concentration required to resolve tophi;
4. The selection, pharmacogenomic screening, and renal starting dose titration schedule for first-line urate-lowering therapy (allopurinol vs. febuxostat);
5. The design of a concomitant anti-inflammatory flare prophylaxis regimen that safely bridges the early months of urate-lowering therapy in advanced CKD without causing further nephrotoxicity or drug accumulation.

